# Line endings, decided here rather than by whoever checked this out.
#
# install.sh is the file the one-line install fetches and hands to `sh` on a
# machine that has nothing else. With CRLF endings the shell reads the carriage
# return as part of the last word on the line and refuses `set -eu` as an
# illegal option — an installer that is correct in the repository and broken on
# the target machine. It has happened once already, on the first Linux release.
#
# The archives are binary and text=auto leaves them alone.
* text=auto eol=lf

# PowerShell reads either, and install.ps1 is the one a person might open in
# Notepad on a machine with nothing installed.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Nothing here may be normalised on the way in or out: a byte changed in an
# archive is an archive that fails its own checksum.
*.tar.gz -text
*.zip -text
*.sha256 text eol=lf
